 1 big thing: Pay-for-play text rocks White House 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Photo illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Photos via Getty Images Top Trump adviser Ric Grenell allegedly outlined a stunning pay-for-influence offer to a Turkish billionaire last year, Axios' Marc Caputo reports exclusively. 

 The offer to Turkish businessman Turhan Mildon included access to "POTUS," "VIP events" and administration officials, according to a text message that the White House counsel is investigating. 🤳 The May 3, 2025, text to Mildon says: "Once the money comes to the company here, I will arrange the donation to Kennedy Centre [sic], and you'll be joining the VIP events and get close with the administration, even with POTUS."

 Administration officials say the text is key evidence in the White House's investigation of whether Grenell misappropriated a potential multimillion-dollar donation to the Kennedy Center, which he led at the time.

 Mildon is married to another Trump adviser: Alina Habba, former U.S. attorney for New Jersey. She stormed into the Oval Office on Oct. 9 and accused Grenell of hoodwinking her husband into making a Kennedy Center donation that never arrived, Axios first reported . Grenell has called the accusations "lies" and denied sending the text message. 💵 At the heart of the alleged scheme is a financial transaction involving a Houston-based defense company's software and the Democratic Republic of Congo, Mildon and Habba's attorneys have told the White House.

 Mildon alleges that at Grenell's direction, he wired $17.6 million to Texas Armament &amp; Technology. Mildon says the money was to be split in two parts: one for the Congo deal and nearly $6 million for the Kennedy Center. Habba told Trump it took 17 months for Mildon to learn the Kennedy Center never received the money he thought was included in the wire sent at Grenell's direction.

 It's unclear what the Congo contract was for, how it related to the Kennedy Center or what the financial specifics were. Keep reading . 

 
 
